# TypeScript & React

## TypeScript

- Prefer `strict` mode. Avoid `any`; use `unknown` + narrowing when needed.
- Prefer `type` for unions/intersections; `interface` for extendable object shapes used by components/APIs.
- Export shared types from a clear module (`types.ts` or domain folder) — no duplicate inline shapes.
- Use discriminated unions for state machines and API result variants.

## React

- Functional components only. Extract reusable logic into custom hooks (`useX`).
- Keep components focused: presentational UI vs data/container concerns.
- Derive state when possible; avoid syncing props into state unless intentional.
- Prefer controlled inputs for forms unless a library (e.g. React Hook Form) is already in use.
- Keys must be stable and unique — never array index for dynamic lists that reorder.

## Imports & Modules

- Prefer absolute imports (`@/`) when the project configures them.
- Do not export default from utility modules; named exports improve discoverability.
- Client/server boundary: do not import server-only modules into Client Components.

## Errors & UX

- Surface actionable error messages to users; log technical detail server-side.
- Loading and empty states are required for async UI that users wait on.
